# DESCRIPTION

dptx drives the DisplayPort transmitter behind each of the CIX Sky1's display engines (a Trilinear DPTX core) by reading and watching it only.
The link firmware trained stays as it is: dptx resets nothing, trains nothing, and leaves the PHY and clocks alone.
devmgr(8) starts one for each transmitter:

```text
driver dptx
class link
match acpi CIXH502F
```

The transmitter is in its engine's power domain, and a register read of an unpowered one stops the machine.
devmgr pairs each transmitter with its engine, the n-th of each by register address, and mounts the engine's platform directory at dptx's `/dev/engine`.
Unless the engine's `power` there says on, dptx reads no register.

Otherwise it probes the transmitter: it must be enabled, with an AUX clock divider firmware set; the link firmware trained gives its rate and lanes, and the PHY's lanes ready.
Over the AUX channel, one request of at most 16 bytes at a time, polled, DEFERs retried, it reads the sink's receiver capabilities (DPCD `0x000`-`0x00F`) and its sink count (`0x200`), and, where a monitor is there, its EDID through I2C-over-AUX (E-DDC: address `0x50`, the segment pointer `0x30` past two blocks), up to four blocks.
On the Orange Pi 6 Plus, DP4 feeds a PS185, a DisplayPort-to-HDMI converter: a branch, whose sink count is the HDMI monitor behind it, and whose EDID comes through.

HPD's level changes and the sink's short pulses come on the transmitter's interrupt, the others masked.
Each reads the sink again, and a change of HPD, the sink count or the EDID is told to the link's clients.

The link is `vectraos.display.link`'s `Link` (display-link-link(5)): `sink` says whether a monitor is there (HPD high and, behind a branch, one counted) and the sink count, `edid` gives the EDID, and the event `sinkChanged` says to ask again.
devmgr mounts dptx's tree at the paired engine's `/dev/link`, and linlon(3) follows it.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/dptx-acpi-_SB_.DP0<n>`.

: `link`
  The link's service.
: `edid`
  Read only: the EDID in hex, 16 bytes a line; empty without one.
: `status`
  Read only.
  Either `off: ...`, where the engine is unpowered or unknown and no register was read, or the probe's lines: `its engine powered by firmware`; `core revision <hex>, transmitter enabled, aux divider <n>`; `link <rate> x<lanes>, sources <hex>, phy lanes ready <hex>`; `watching hpd` (or `no interrupt: hpd not watched`).
  Where the probe stopped, its last line is `idle: ...` or `FAILED: <why>`.
  Then the sink: `hpd low: no sink`, or `hpd high`, `dpcd <revision> <rate> x<lanes>[, enhanced framing][, branch], sink count <n>`, `edid <n> bytes: <maker> <name>, preferred <w>x<h> at <hz> Hz` or `no edid`, and `aux failed: <why>` if a read failed; and `hpd events <n>, pulses <n>, changes <n>; aux requests <n>, timeouts <n>`.

